Penny Goblin is a household ledger for **Linux and Android**, built with Zig and
Oriel. It is in early development. The application's source repository is
private; its builds and this website are public.

# Try a build

Download the latest build:

- [Linux x86_64 AppImage](https://github.com/highercomve/penny-goblin-site/releases/latest/download/penny-goblin-linux-x86_64-oriel.AppImage) (compiled Oriel interface; the scanner can use any GPU through Vulkan)
- [Linux x86_64 AppImage, WebView interface](https://github.com/highercomve/penny-goblin-site/releases/latest/download/penny-goblin-linux-x86_64-webview.AppImage) (scans on NVIDIA GPUs with CUDA)
- [Windows x86_64 installer](https://github.com/highercomve/penny-goblin-site/releases/latest/download/penny-goblin-windows-x86_64-setup.exe)
- [macOS disk image, Apple silicon](https://github.com/highercomve/penny-goblin-site/releases/latest/download/penny-goblin-macos-arm64.dmg)
- [Android arm64 APK](https://github.com/highercomve/penny-goblin-site/releases/latest/download/penny-goblin-android-arm64.apk)

Every version, its notes and the [SHA256SUMS](https://github.com/highercomve/penny-goblin-site/releases/latest/download/SHA256SUMS)
of its files are on the [releases page](https://github.com/highercomve/penny-goblin-site/releases).
There is no app-store release yet.

Linux builds need compatible GTK4 and WebKitGTK 6 system libraries; make the
AppImage executable (`chmod +x`) before running it. The WebView AppImage
scans receipts on an NVIDIA GPU when the NVIDIA driver and the CUDA 13
runtime (cuBLAS) are installed, and on the CPU otherwise. Windows 10 and 11
need the WebView2 runtime (included with Windows 11 and current Edge); the
scanner can use the GPU through Vulkan (Settings → Receipt scanner). The macOS
build is for Apple silicon (macOS 13 or newer) and scans with Metal; it isn't
notarized, so open it the first time with right-click → Open. Android builds require
Android 10 or newer. These APKs use development signing: an existing
installation can only be upgraded by an APK with the same certificate. Keep
your current data and pairing safe; use an isolated test device or profile when
trying differently signed builds.

# Your first payment

1. Choose Scan, then take a photo or select a receipt or transfer voucher.
2. Check the total after discounts, merchant, date and account. Correct any mistakes.
3. Save the reviewed payment. Explore its month, category and account from the ledger.

Scanner models are optional, downloaded separately and not included with the app.
Manual entry remains available. Scan accuracy and speed depend on the image,
device and selected model.

# Pair your devices

Open pairing and synchronization on your desktop and pair the phone using its
QR code or pairing link. The app authenticates and pins the desktop certificate.
Use the same reachable local network, or connect through Tailscale.

Once paired, the phone can ask the desktop to scan an image or research
uncategorized merchants. Category research uses the current period and payment
filters. Its suggestions remain a draft until you review and select Apply.

# Build it yourself

Collaborators can clone the private repository and follow its README. The source
contains a documented Oriel snapshot so builds do not depend on a developer's
local framework checkout. Zig 0.16.0 is required.

```sh
zig build run -Drenderer=oriel
bash scripts/build-linux.sh oriel
python3 scripts/build-android.py
```

For Android, install JDK 17, Gradle 8.14.3 and the SDK/NDK versions listed in the
README first. The project website is built with Zine 0.14.0.
